6. FIRESTORE DATA ARCHITECTURE

The final database design must follow the existing repository and Firestore architecture.

The following is a logical reference model, not permission to create duplicate collections if equivalent systems already exist.

6.1 Recommended logical structure

conversations/{conversationId}
    members/{userId}
    messages/{messageId}
        reactions/{reactionId}
        deliveryReceipts/{userId}

users/{userId}/messageInbox/{conversationId}

users/{userId}/messageRequests/{requestId}

users/{userId}/messageDrafts/{draftId}

users/{userId}/messageViews/{viewId}

Adapt the structure to existing collection conventions.

Avoid unnecessary duplication between users, conversations, messages and existing notification or social graph collections.

6.2 Conversation identity

One-to-one conversations must not be created repeatedly for the same two users.

Use a deterministic, server-validated identity strategy based on the two authenticated user IDs.

The backend must canonicalize participant ordering before generating or resolving the conversation identity.

The creation operation must be idempotent.

Two concurrent attempts to create the same direct conversation must resolve to one authorized conversation rather than two separate conversations.

Never accept a client-supplied participant list as sufficient authorization.

6.3 Conversation document

A logical conversation may contain:

interface DirectConversation {
  id: string;
  type: "direct";
  participantIds: [string, string];
  createdBy: string;
  createdAt: Timestamp;
  updatedAt: Timestamp;
  lastMessageAt?: Timestamp;
  lastMessageId?: string;
  lastMessagePreview?: string;
  status: "active" | "restricted" | "closed";
}

Adjust this interface to the existing project language, serialization conventions and actual Firestore schema.

Do not copy unnecessary private profile data into conversation documents.

Any denormalized display information must be treated as a disposable, refreshable UI snapshot rather than the authoritative profile record.

6.4 Conversation membership

Keep per-user conversation state separate from shared conversation data.

A logical membership record can contain:

interface DirectConversationMember {
  userId: string;
  status: "active" | "blocked" | "left";
  joinedAt: Timestamp;
  lastReadAt?: Timestamp;
  lastReadMessageId?: string;
  lastDeliveredAt?: Timestamp;
  lastDeliveredMessageId?: string;
}

Do not let users assign themselves arbitrary roles, identities or permissions.

Users must not be able to modify another user's membership state.

6.5 Message model

Use an explicit message type.

Example logical structure:

type MessageType =
  | "text"
  | "image"
  | "video"
  | "audio"
  | "voice_note"
  | "document"
  | "link"
  | "shared_content"
  | "system";

interface DirectMessage {
  id: string;
  conversationId: string;
  senderId: string;
  type: MessageType;
  text?: string;
  attachment?: MessageAttachment;
  replyToMessageId?: string;
  sharedContent?: SharedContentReference;
  forwardedFrom?: ForwardedMessageReference;
  createdAt: Timestamp;
  editedAt?: Timestamp;
  deletedAt?: Timestamp;
}

Use a discriminated model where appropriate so each message type has its own validated payload.

A message cannot contain an arbitrary mixture of unrelated payload fields.

A text message must not be accepted as a malformed video message.

A shared-content message must not be accepted without a valid reference and content type.

6.6 Inbox summaries

A user's inbox should not require downloading every historical message.

Maintain efficient per-user conversation summaries.

Update summaries through a trusted, idempotent backend pipeline or another properly validated existing architecture.

Do not let a client arbitrarily manipulate unread counters, conversation ordering or last-message metadata.

Inbox summaries must remain consistent with the canonical conversation and message records.

6.7 Message pagination

Implement cursor-based pagination.

Requirements:

- Fetch a bounded initial message page.
- Load older messages on demand.
- Preserve chronological ordering.
- Avoid repeatedly loading existing messages.
- Avoid loading an entire conversation history.
- Preserve scroll position when earlier messages load.
- Avoid unnecessary listeners for old messages.
- Unsubscribe from listeners when switching conversations or leaving the screen.

Select page sizes based on actual application behavior and performance measurements. A reasonable starting point is approximately 30–50 messages per page.

Do not use unbounded Firestore queries.

6.8 Data consistency

Handle:

- Duplicate sends.
- Concurrent sends.
- Rapid repeated actions.
- Out-of-order event delivery.
- Stale inbox summaries.
- Interrupted uploads.
- Deleted referenced messages.
- Deleted referenced posts or Vibes.
- Duplicate Cloud Function invocations.
- Network retries.
- User account changes.

Message creation, inbox summary updates and notification processing must have clearly defined consistency responsibilities.

Use transactions, atomic operations or idempotency keys wherever the operation requires them.

Do not rely on client timestamps as the authoritative ordering mechanism when a server-generated timestamp is available.


