11. IMAGES, VIDEO, AUDIO AND FILE ATTACHMENTS

Implement a secure, reliable media pipeline.

11.1 Images

Support:

- Image selection.
- Preview.
- Upload progress.
- Appropriate image format validation.
- Image rendering.
- Thumbnail or preview generation when existing infrastructure supports it.
- Full-screen image viewing.
- Multiple-image sending within sensible application limits.
- Retry after upload interruption.
- Error placeholders.
- Media removal according to authorization.

Do not load original high-resolution media unnecessarily in conversation lists.

Use appropriate thumbnail and display sizes.

Preserve image aspect ratios.

11.2 Video

Support:

- Video selection.
- Preview.
- Video format validation.
- Duration validation.
- File-size validation.
- Upload progress.
- Playback controls.
- Loading and playback errors.
- Appropriate video thumbnails where supported.
- Full-screen playback.

Do not automatically play every video in the message timeline.

Avoid downloading full video files simply to render a small preview.

Do not treat a video as uploaded before the upload has actually completed.

11.3 Audio attachments

Support normal audio-file attachments where appropriate.

Show:

- File name.
- Audio type.
- Playback state.
- Duration when available.
- Error state.
- Download or save behavior only when supported.

11.4 Voice notes

Voice notes are an important Part 1 feature.

Implement:

- Microphone permission request.
- Permission-denied state.
- Recording start.
- Recording stop.
- Recording cancellation.
- Duration display.
- Recording feedback.
- Audio preview before sending.
- Playback preview.
- Upload progress.
- Failed upload handling.
- Retry.
- Safe cancellation.
- Cleanup of temporary recordings.
- Accessible controls.

Do not leave microphone recording active after the user exits or cancels the recording flow.

Do not claim that a voice note has been sent before persistence is confirmed.

Support the device/browser capabilities available to the current application.

Handle unsupported browsers gracefully.

Do not silently fall back to a fake recording interface.

11.5 Documents and supported files

Support carefully selected file types.

Validate:

- MIME type.
- File extension.
- File size.
- Upload destination.
- Ownership.
- Conversation authorization.
- Associated message.
- Download authorization.

Client-side validation is for user experience, not a security boundary.

Validate again on the trusted backend where required.

Prevent arbitrary executable content from being treated as a harmless document.

Do not provide unsafe automatic file execution or preview behavior.

11.6 Upload reliability

Use the project's existing Firebase Storage integration where possible.

Implement:

- Resumable upload where supported.
- Progress reporting.
- Cancellation.
- Retry.
- Upload timeout handling.
- Network interruption recovery.
- Temporary-file cleanup.
- Orphaned-upload cleanup.
- Attachment ownership verification.

Use a logical ownership-aware path, adapted to existing conventions, such as:

messages/{conversationId}/{messageId}/{attachmentId}

Only authorized conversation participants should be able to read protected media.

A user must not gain access to an attachment simply by guessing its URL or Storage path.

Do not expose unprotected permanent media URLs as a substitute for authorization.

Do not claim antivirus scanning or advanced content analysis unless an actual integrated security service performs it.

11.7 Resource management

Prevent:

- Unreleased object URLs.
- Unreleased MediaRecorder instances.
- Persistent microphone activity.
- Unnecessary memory retention.
- Unbounded video playback.
- Large media downloads in background lists.
- Duplicate upload listeners.
- Stale upload callbacks after navigation.

Clean up every resource correctly.


