14. MESSAGE REQUESTS AND COMMUNICATION PERMISSIONS

Message requests must be part of the real social graph and privacy architecture.

They must not become a separate, competing relationship system.

14.1 Communication eligibility

Use the existing social graph and privacy model.

Inspect the current meanings of:

- Friends.
- Followers.
- Connections.
- Blocked users.
- Muted users.
- Restricted users.
- Other existing relationship states.

Do not invent new relationship categories if the existing system already defines them.

14.2 Message privacy settings

Integrate messaging with the existing privacy settings system.

Support clear choices appropriate to the actual Arvdoul relationship model, such as:

- People permitted by the existing relationship rules.
- People who can send message requests.
- More restrictive messaging permissions.

Only expose settings that are actually enforced by the backend.

Client-side button visibility is not authorization.

A user must not be able to bypass another user's messaging preferences through a direct function call or fabricated Firestore request.

14.3 Request experience

The recipient must be able to:

- View a request.
- See the sender's permitted profile information.
- Preview the initial message.
- Accept the request.
- Decline the request.
- Block the sender.
- Report the request.

The sender must receive an accurate representation of the request state.

Do not expose private recipient information before the recipient accepts the request.

Do not allow rejected or blocked users to bypass restrictions by repeatedly creating new conversations.

14.4 Abuse prevention

Introduce reasonable protections against:

- Request flooding.
- Repeated unsolicited messages.
- Automated submission.
- Duplicate requests.
- Malformed payloads.
- Unusual message volume.
- Attempts to circumvent blocks.

Use trusted backend enforcement where necessary.

Do not rely exclusively on client-side cooldowns.

Do not introduce arbitrary restrictions that conflict with existing product policies without documenting them.


