20. SECURITY AND FIREBASE HARDENING

Security is a core acceptance requirement.

A polished interface cannot compensate for an insecure messaging backend.

20.1 Authentication

Every protected messaging operation must use an authenticated user identity.

Never trust a client-supplied user ID as proof of identity.

Validate the authenticated UID against the operation and requested resources.

Handle expired or invalid authentication safely.

20.2 Firestore Security Rules

Inspect and improve existing security rules without overwriting unrelated application permissions.

Rules must enforce:

- Authenticated access.
- Valid conversation membership.
- Exactly two participants for Part 1 direct conversations.
- No arbitrary participant injection.
- No sender impersonation.
- No unauthorized message reads.
- No unauthorized message writes.
- Correct message ownership.
- Restricted edits.
- Restricted deletion.
- Correct reaction ownership.
- Protected read and delivery cursors.
- Protected inbox summary updates.
- Protected request transitions.
- Protected conversation settings.
- Valid document fields and expected types.

Users must not update another user's inbox or membership record.

Users must not set arbitrary delivery or read states.

Users must not modify authoritative last-message metadata if it is server-controlled.

Test the security rules with authenticated and unauthenticated scenarios.

20.3 Storage Security Rules

Enforce:

- Authenticated access.
- Valid path ownership.
- Conversation membership.
- Attachment association.
- File size limits.
- Allowed content types.
- Correct authorization for reads.
- Correct authorization for deletes.
- No arbitrary user-to-user data access.

Do not rely on client-side file validation.

Do not allow someone to upload an arbitrary file and assign it to a message they do not own.

20.4 Cloud Functions security

For every messaging-related backend function:

- Validate authentication.
- Validate inputs.
- Validate resource ownership.
- Validate participant permissions.
- Apply appropriate rate limiting.
- Use idempotency where needed.
- Avoid trusting client-supplied counters.
- Avoid trusting client timestamps for authoritative state.
- Avoid logging message content.
- Return safe, structured errors.
- Avoid exposing internal infrastructure details.
- Keep secrets in approved secret-management mechanisms.

20.5 Abuse and rate limiting

Introduce appropriate limits for:

- Message creation.
- Message requests.
- Attachment uploads.
- Message edits.
- Reports.
- Search requests.
- Expensive operations.

Choose thresholds based on the product's usage model and observed behavior.

Do not invent limits that make normal communication unusable.

Critical enforcement must happen on a trusted backend.

20.6 Privacy and encryption

Part 1 must not claim to provide end-to-end encryption unless it actually implements and verifies a genuine E2EE protocol.

Firebase encryption at rest is not end-to-end encryption.

Do not display fake encrypted-chat badges, fake private-key indicators or misleading encryption claims.

Use the existing platform's supported transport security and Firebase security controls.

Design the data model and service boundaries so a future independently reviewed encryption architecture can be introduced without rewriting the presentation layer.

Do not implement custom cryptography.

20.7 Screenshot limitations

Do not claim that the application prevents screenshots or guarantees that private content cannot be copied.

Implement only controls that the actual platform supports and explain their limitations if exposed to the user.


