24. PERFORMANCE AND SCALABILITY

Design for real usage, not a handful of test conversations.

24.1 Firestore performance

Avoid:

- Unbounded message queries.
- Repeated reads of the same profile.
- Repeated full conversation fetches.
- Excessive real-time listeners.
- Unnecessary per-message receipt writes.
- Large documents.
- Growing arrays.
- Duplicate notification queries.
- Excessive writes caused by UI state changes.

Use:

- Bounded queries.
- Cursor pagination.
- Appropriate indexes.
- Minimal listener scope.
- Efficient inbox summaries.
- Local caching where safe.
- Server timestamps.
- Batched operations where appropriate.
- Transactions for operations requiring atomicity.
- Idempotent backend functions.

24.2 Firestore indexes

Inspect existing indexes.

Add only indexes required by real queries.

Ensure indexes cover:

- Conversation inbox queries.
- Conversation message timelines.
- Message search strategy where applicable.
- Message requests.
- Shared-media queries.
- Other implemented query patterns.

Do not claim indexes were deployed unless deployment was actually performed.

24.3 React performance

Avoid:

- Rendering the entire message history.
- Recreating expensive listeners on every render.
- Updating every message bubble when one message changes.
- Unnecessary global state subscriptions.
- Excessive context updates.
- Unnecessary media decoding.
- Large components doing unrelated work.
- Memory leaks from listeners and media resources.

Use the existing state management architecture.

Apply memoization and virtualization where measurement or data volume justifies them.

Do not add virtualization complexity to small components without a clear need.

24.4 Media performance

Use:

- Appropriate image variants.
- Lazy loading.
- Thumbnail previews.
- Correct media sizing.
- Controlled video playback.
- Efficient caching.
- Resource cleanup.

Never download original media unnecessarily just to render a small inbox preview.

24.5 Cost awareness

Evaluate the cost impact of:

- Firestore reads.
- Firestore writes.
- Storage operations.
- Cloud Function invocations.
- FCM requests.
- Listener fan-out.
- Search operations.

Do not introduce a feature that generates multiple writes for every small UI action without justification.

Document important cost trade-offs.


